CAT = "arrtmriSeq"; SEG = "SEQ"
_nyq = 2 * 16; assert _nyq == 32
Q = [
("In a conventional spin echo, what does the 180-degree pulse do?",
 "Refocuses fixed-field dephasing", ["Tips the magnetization into the transverse plane", "Encodes position along the readout axis", "Suppresses the fat signal"],
 "The 180-degree pulse flips the dephasing spins so that the faster ones catch up, forming a spin echo at time TE and removing the T2* part of the decay."),
("Why is fat bright on fast spin echo T2 images, compared with conventional spin echo?",
 "Rapid 180-degree pulses limit J-coupling", ["Fat has a long T1 at every field", "The readout gradients null the water", "Fat absorbs more of the gadolinium"],
 "The rapid train of 180-degree pulses suppresses the J-coupling that normally shortens fat's T2, so fat stays bright. Fat suppression is often added to FSE T2 images."),
("A STIR sequence begins with which RF pulse?",
 "A 180-degree inversion pulse", ["A 90-degree pulse alone", "A low flip angle gradient pulse", "A spectral fat pulse"],
 "Inversion recovery starts with a 180-degree pulse, waits for TI, then images. In STIR the short TI is timed to the null point of fat."),
("How does a gradient echo form its echo?",
 "By reversing the readout gradient", ["By a 180-degree refocusing RF pulse", "By a second 90-degree pulse", "By an inversion pulse"],
 "A gradient echo dephases the spins with one gradient lobe and rephases them by reversing it. With no 180-degree pulse, fixed inhomogeneities are not refocused, so contrast is T2*."),
("In a spoiled gradient echo, what happens to the transverse magnetization left at the end of each TR?",
 "It is destroyed before the next pulse", ["It is kept and added to the next echo", "It is converted into longitudinal magnetization", "It is refocused into a spin echo"],
 "Spoiling with gradients or RF phase removes leftover transverse signal, so contrast is driven by T1. Coherent gradient echoes keep it, which adds T2 effects."),
("Balanced steady state free precession images make fluid and blood very bright. Which ratio sets the contrast?",
 "T2/T1", ["T1 alone", "Proton density alone", "Diffusion coefficient"],
 "In balanced SSFP the signal depends on the ratio T2/T1, which is high for fluid and blood. It is fast and used for cardiac cine and fluid imaging."),
("Why does time-of-flight MRA show flowing blood as bright?",
 "Unsaturated blood flows into the slice", ["Blood has a short T1 like fat", "Gadolinium is always injected first", "The gradients slow the blood down"],
 "Stationary tissue is saturated by repeated pulses, while inflowing blood has full magnetization and gives strong signal: inflow enhancement."),
("For TOF MRA of the carotid arteries, where is a saturation band placed to remove venous signal?",
 "Above the slices", ["Below the slices", "Over the face only", "In the slice center"],
 "Jugular venous blood flows down into the neck, so a band above the imaging stack saturates it while arterial blood entering from below stays bright."),
("Why is TOF MRA slab orientation set perpendicular to the main vessel?",
 "In-plane flow gets saturated", ["Perpendicular slabs need no gradients", "It removes all motion artifact", "It halves the dose of contrast"],
 "Blood running within the slab stays in it long enough to be saturated. Flow through the slab brings fresh spins each TR."),
("How does phase contrast MRA create vessel signal?",
 "From phase shifts of moving spins", ["From the inflow of unsaturated spins", "From an injection of gadolinium", "From nulling fat with an inversion pulse"],
 "Bipolar gradients leave stationary spins with no net phase and give moving spins a phase shift proportional to velocity, so flow direction and speed can be measured."),
("Contrast-enhanced MRA is commonly acquired with which type of sequence?",
 "A fast 3D spoiled gradient echo", ["A 2D conventional spin echo", "A single-shot FSE", "A spectroscopy sequence"],
 "A 3D T1-weighted spoiled gradient echo with very short TR and TE catches the gadolinium bolus as it passes through the arteries."),
("Smooth flow with fastest velocity in the center of a vessel and slowest at the walls is called what?",
 "Laminar flow", ["Turbulent flow", "Vortex flow", "Stagnant flow"],
 "Laminar flow has a parabolic velocity profile. Turbulent flow, as past a stenosis, has random velocities and causes signal loss."),
("Which sequence can fill all of k-space after a single RF excitation by oscillating the readout gradient?",
 "Single-shot echo planar imaging", ["Conventional spin echo", "Spoiled gradient echo", "Inversion recovery spin echo"],
 "Single-shot EPI acquires every line in one shot, freezing motion, which is why it is used for diffusion and perfusion. It suffers from distortion and susceptibility artifact."),
("An acute stroke region is bright on DWI and dark on the ADC map. What does this show?",
 "Restricted diffusion", ["T2 shine-through", "Normal free water", "Calcium deposition"],
 "Cytotoxic edema limits water motion, so DWI signal is high and the ADC is low. T2 shine-through is bright on DWI but not dark on ADC."),
("A lesion is bright on DWI and also bright on the ADC map. What explains the DWI brightness?",
 "T2 shine-through", ["Restricted diffusion", "Blood flow", "Fat in the lesion"],
 "DWI has T2 weighting underneath, so a very long-T2 lesion can be bright with free diffusion. The bright ADC confirms diffusion is not restricted."),
("Which sequence is most sensitive to small hemorrhages and calcium in the brain?",
 "Susceptibility weighted imaging", ["Fast spin echo T2 imaging", "STIR inversion recovery imaging", "Proton density spin echo"],
 "SWI is a high-resolution gradient echo that uses phase information to boost susceptibility effects from blood products, venous blood and calcium."),
("A brain perfusion study tracks signal loss as a gadolinium bolus passes. What is this method called?",
 "Dynamic susceptibility contrast", ["Arterial spin labeling", "Time-of-flight angiography", "Phase contrast velocity mapping"],
 "DSC perfusion uses fast T2* EPI to follow the drop in signal as the bolus passes. Arterial spin labeling magnetically labels blood instead of using contrast."),
("Which perfusion method needs no injected contrast?",
 "Arterial spin labeling", ["Dynamic susceptibility contrast", "Dynamic contrast-enhanced T1 imaging", "Contrast-enhanced MRA"],
 "ASL inverts the magnetization of inflowing arterial blood in the neck, which acts as an endogenous tracer."),
("On proton MR spectroscopy of the brain, which metabolite peak near 2.0 ppm marks healthy neurons?",
 "N-acetylaspartate", ["Choline", "Lactate", "Lipids"],
 "NAA is a neuronal marker and falls when neurons are lost. Choline (near 3.2 ppm) rises with membrane turnover, as in many tumors."),
("On brain spectroscopy, an inverted doublet near 1.3 ppm at an intermediate TE of about 144 ms suggests which metabolite?",
 "Lactate", ["Creatine", "Myo-inositol", "NAA"],
 "Lactate's doublet inverts at TE about 144 ms, which separates it from lipid. It signals anaerobic metabolism, as in ischemia or necrotic tumor."),
("Centric k-space filling acquires which lines first?",
 "The central lines", ["The outer edges", "The top lines only", "Random lines"],
 "Centric ordering fills the center first so contrast is captured at a chosen moment, as at the peak of a contrast bolus."),
("Which k-space method reuses the outer lines from an earlier dynamic frame and updates only the center?",
 "Keyhole imaging", ["Spiral filling", "Linear filling", "Half Fourier"],
 "Keyhole imaging repeatedly refreshes the central contrast-bearing lines, speeding dynamic studies such as contrast uptake."),
("What turns raw k-space data into an image?",
 "A fast Fourier transform", ["A maximum intensity projection", "Bolus tracking", "Window and level"],
 "The two-dimensional Fourier transform converts frequency and phase data into spatial positions and intensities."),
("A signal contains frequencies up to 16 kHz. Under the Nyquist theorem, what is the lowest sampling rate that captures it without aliasing?",
 "32 kHz", ["16 kHz", "8 kHz", "64 kHz"],
 "The sampling rate must be at least twice the highest frequency: 2 x 16 = 32 kHz. Sampling more slowly assigns signal to the wrong frequency."),
("Which postprocessing calculates a map of how freely water moves, using images taken at two or more b-values?",
 "ADC mapping", ["Maximum intensity projection", "Multiplanar reformation", "Image subtraction"],
 "The apparent diffusion coefficient map is calculated from the signal change between b-values. It removes T2 shine-through, so restricted diffusion is dark on the map."),
("A precontrast 3D volume is subtracted from the matching postcontrast volume. What does this show?",
 "Only tissue that enhanced", ["Only the fat", "Only the cortical bone", "Only the background noise"],
 "Subtraction removes stationary background signal so enhancing lesions or vessels stand out. Patient motion between the scans spoils it."),
("Which postprocessing builds coronal and sagittal images from a thin 3D volume?",
 "Multiplanar reformation", ["Maximum intensity projection", "Bolus tracking", "Parallel imaging"],
 "MPR reslices a volume in any plane. Thin isotropic voxels give reformats of the same quality as the source."),
("A CINE display is used for which kind of study?",
 "Showing motion through a cycle", ["Measuring the diffusion of water", "Plotting the metabolite peaks", "Checking field homogeneity"],
 "CINE plays a series of images in a loop, as for cardiac function, CSF flow or a TMJ opening and closing."),
("Which part of a stored MR image file holds the patient name, study date and scan parameters?",
 "The DICOM header", ["The pixel data", "The HL7 message", "The k-space raw data"],
 "A DICOM file has a header with patient, study and acquisition data and the pixel data itself. Wrong header data can file images under the wrong patient."),
("Which messaging standard commonly carries orders and reports between the hospital system and the RIS?",
 "HL7", ["DICOM", "JPEG", "TCP/IP"],
 "Health Level Seven (HL7) carries text data such as orders, admissions and reports. DICOM carries the images themselves."),
("A technologist steps away from the console during a busy shift. What protects patient information?",
 "Lock the workstation", ["Leave the log-in for the next user", "Turn off the monitor only", "Post the password on the console"],
 "Locking the screen or logging off keeps the session private. Shared or posted passwords break HIPAA security safeguards and the audit trail."),
("Images were sent to the wrong patient folder on the archive. What is the right next step?",
 "Report it so it is corrected", ["Delete the study and rescan", "Leave the images in place", "Copy them to both patients"],
 "Misfiled images are corrected by the PACS administrator under the facility's procedure, and the event is documented, because the wrong record can harm care and privacy."),
]
